  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 116 AM EDT Fri Jul 17 2026\n\n- Reduced Visibilities and Poor Air Quality From Smoke Friday\n\nFor much of the day conditions will be similar to Thursday. \nWidespread near-surface smoke likely leads to visbys around a mile \nor less. Leaning on the RRFS-Smoke guidance which has handled things \nwell suggests that dense smoke is possible near daybreak, \nparticularly near US-10 with visbys of under 1/2 mile. Will \nmonitor for a potential Special Weather Statement or Dense Smoke \nAdvisory if this threat materializes, though confidence is low at \nthis time in dense smoke developing. Conditions should begin to \nimprove later Friday into Friday night as southwest flow develops. \nHowever, smoke modeling guidance suggests this may be be short lived \nwith additional smoke potentially arriving behind a cold front \nSaturday. Air quality is also expected to remain at hazardous levels \nFriday as well. See the latest Air Quality Alert from Michigan's \nDept. of Environment, Great Lakes, and Energy for details.\n\n- Chances of Showers and Thunderstorms Friday Through Saturday\n\nA few chances for showers and storms exist between late this \nafternoon/evening and Saturday. First is today's potential which is \nassociated first with a weak wave that may touch off a few showers \nacross Central Michigan this morning. The threat region then shifts \nsouth this afternoon/evening as a warm front arrives. MUCAPE north \nof 2000 J/kg supports both showers and thunderstorms. However, \nkinematics are poor with deep layer shear around 20 knots. This will \nkeep any storms pulsey.\n\nA shortwave then drives a cold front south across the area Saturday \nwith showers and storms expected to develop across this front. The \nfront should clear the area early Sunday afternoon, however if we \ncan get enough surface based instability development, organized \nclusters with strong to severe winds are possible given mid-level \nflow of 40 kts with ample DCAPE. The better chance is south of our \narea where instability is better.\n\n- Additional Showers and Storms Possible Monday into Tuesday\n\nHigh pressure moves into the region behind the front keeping things \ndry for Sunday ahead of the next system. By later Monday into \nTuesday, a series of shortwaves will drive fronts across the area, \nculminating in a cold front Monday Night into Tuesday. Forecast \ninstability suggests showers and thunderstorms are possible. \nNorthwest flow then marks a cooldown expected into mid-week with \nhighs falling to near 80.",
